Survival analysis: zero mean, 42% ruin, and an unmeasurable edge

Tests the only remaining case for PTC - that its 9x variance reduction is
worth something even when the mean is indistinguishable from zero.

METHOD. The edge is not assumed. Each simulated account draws its own true
per-contract edge from the measured posterior (N(+0.02c, 0.80c), from the
day-clustered population CI of [-1.57, +1.62]) and trades under it, so the
uncertainty propagates into the answer instead of hiding behind a point
estimate. 40,000 paths per cell, streamed in chunks.

RESULT at $700 over 90 days:

  CONTROL   P(ruin) 0.4188   median $702.69   5th pct  -$385.22
  PTC       P(ruin) 0.0000   median $700.02   5th pct  +$586.29

Both medians land on the starting equity, which is what a zero mean looks
like. CONTROL reaches that median while dying 42% of the time. PTC survives,
but stakes so much less that nothing happens in either direction.

Variance reduction here is not free performance - it is a smaller bet. PTC is
not a way to make the strategy profitable; it is a way to stay solvent long
enough to find out whether it is.

THE FINISHER - how long until the edge is measurable at all:

  detect +0.5c/contract   CONTROL  9,988 days   PTC  55,739 days
  detect +1.0c/contract   CONTROL  2,497 days   PTC  13,935 days

Neither policy resolves a half-cent edge on any sane horizon, and PTC is worse
precisely because it stakes less. The edge is not merely unproven, it is
UNMEASURABLE at this account size under either execution.

This closes the loop with the priced-signal result in fa2927c. The probe
signal is real (+21pp, replicated at n=8,201, P=0.0000) and already priced, so
there is no edge to collect; and the variance reduction that remains cannot
manufacture one, only postpone the outcome.

